<p>As the Iran–US–Israel conflict escalates, new analyses and explainers describe Tehran as settling into a long game: a protracted endurance campaign meant to outlast outside pressure by steadily raising military, economic and political costs, not by chasing a quick battlefield decision. Reports point to a blend of continued missile-and-drone attacks, reliance on regional relationships, and the option of pressuring energy transit routes—moves aimed at amplifying market turmoil and political strain on Washington and its partners. Separately, Al Jazeera says Iran’s long-war doctrine is built to absorb shocks and keep command functioning even under leadership-targeting (“decapitation”) threats—treating time itself as a strategic weapon.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/jsx-qvU14WdDEZojx0ZW5vAIVxKDXCwptCVKREA1aQENtJpqnMg17R6gIYOQk2yaMuIpybcTLJWa2zrntvC_Ffkx5HWBcecuL9KxEckl5p_0v1mvjG5jHbuH_hRVWimDJ_1k8u4H' alt='Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi speaks in an interview segment.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Cost-imposition logic: A Reuters-based analysis frames Iran’s aim as turning the conflict into an endurance test—pairing drones and missiles with economic pressure meant to jolt global markets and push Washington to “blink” first.</li><li>Continuity planning: Al Jazeera highlights the “Fourth Successor” concept—planning designed to keep command and decision-making intact even if senior figures are targeted.</li><li>Week-one posture: The Hindu says Iran has intensified its response through missile strikes, regional alliances, and broader pressure across West Asia, with no immediate ceasefire in sight and fears of a wider war rising.</li><li>‘Wounded but more dangerous’ framing: The Straits Times cites expert views that damage to Iran does not necessarily limit its ability to sustain an endurance strategy and keep up long-running pressure.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iran foreign ministry: Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi called US strikes a 